### Summary

Max Heap Sort is a comparison-based sorting algorithm that uses a **Max Heap data structure**. In this algorithm, the largest element is placed at the root of the heap. The largest element is then repeatedly removed from the heap and placed at the end of the array. This process continues until all elements are sorted.

The main features of Max Heap Sort are:

* It sorts the elements in **ascending order** when a Max Heap is used.
* Best-case, average-case, and worst-case time complexity is **O(n log n)**.
* Its space complexity is **O(1)** when implemented in-place.
* It does not require an additional array for sorting.
* The execution time can be measured using Python's `time.perf_counter()`.

### Conclusion

Max Heap Sort is an efficient sorting algorithm suitable for handling large amounts of data. Its **O(n log n)** time complexity in all cases makes it more predictable than algorithms such as Bubble Sort. Since it can be performed in-place with **O(1) extra space**, it is also memory efficient. Therefore, Max Heap Sort is a useful algorithm for understanding **heap data structures, sorting techniques, time complexity, and execution-time analysis** in Python.
